I got mine in the other day and it's working just as intended, GPUZ doesn't show any abnormal temperatures when playing Doom Eternal at max settings (still testing with other games, trying DCS: World as its one of the more taxing games in my library) and my power supply doesn't appear like its going to blow up. i might want to replace the power supply at some point but the only issues i had was i needed to update the BIOS myself, otherwise the CPU came soft overclocked at 4.9GHz. i got an i7-10700kf and RTX 3060ti and it plays games beautifully, the case has mesh paneling and fans everywhere so it keeps cool just fine. and my alternative was paying exactly the same amount (approximately $1900 USD)for pretty much the same parts but if i wanted the RTX 3060ti i would have to pay over $2000 to a POS scalper JUST for the GPU.

If you want to trash talk a company purely based off of negative experiences of people that go out of their way to complain that's fine, but just know that every person that complains theres probably 2 whos system worked just fine and didn't have anything to say about it, because the system just working is expected of the company.

 @dunktheball  Back when I had the 380 I used it with an i5 4460, which has a lower tdp (84 vs 105) when compared to the 5900x. I did run The Witcher 3 with that system though, and I remember both GPU and CPU being around 90-100%. Didn't measure exactly how much power my system was using but since it didn't shut down I'm assuming it was well under 550w. Board partners often tend to overstate PSU requirements a bit, I remember XFX recommending a 750W PSU for my 380 when in reality it could probably run just fine with a 500w PSU with the parts I used. 

Nvidia themselves recommend a 600W PSU which is likely already more than you need. Board partner cards will likely use a tad more power than the Founders' edition but again Nvidia will already have overstated the requirements. But I mean if you're really worried about it you could always just use the higher wattage PSU as it does give you more upgrade options later. 

IMO the price difference between the 3060ti and 3070 isn't worth it considering the 3060 ti isn't a lot slower and you don't get any extra VRAM. If you want something better than the 3060ti the only good options IMO get a 3080 or 6800xt

