 @MadafakinRio  Put it this way, if there's 50 video cards at $300 (msrp) in the store, for example sake, say 30 customers buy them for personal use, then there's 20 left in the store at $300. You can easily buy one in stock. Now say 20 scalpers decided to come and buy the rest of them to sell for profit. Now there's 0 left at MSRP, but 20 left, but being sold at $500.

That was a simplistic scenario for easy understanding. Not to also factor in that when you have scalpers involved, there is extra purchases being made from people that have no intention of even wanting it other than to sell. Once again, for sake of an example, if only 30 people were interested out of 50 units, there is plenty left. If you add in the 20 scalpers, now they are all sold out. Obviously it's not directly applicable to a new item that just came out because there are enough people wanting them, but the logic still stands why scalpers suck.

And lastly, bots. Items being purchased in mass (for scalping) before people can even check out.

